<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_drop_property_index.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-droppropertyindex">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-droppropertyindex">
  <primary>DROP PROPERTY INDEX</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DROP PROPERTY INDEX</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DROP PROPERTY INDEX</refname>
  <refpurpose>remove a property index</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
DROP PROPERTY INDEX [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable> [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-droppropertyindex-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP PROPERTY INDEX</command> drops one or more indexes over properties, and
   nothing else.
  </para>

  <para>
   The name is resolved in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname> and cannot be
   qualified. A name with a schema on it is rejected with
   <literal>improper property index name</literal>. To drop an index in another graph, change
   <varname>graph_path</varname> first.
  </para>

  <para>
   Only an index over properties is accepted. Every other index on a label is refused with
   <literal>"<replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable>" is not property
   index</literal>: the label's primary key, an edge label's endpoint indexes, and an index
   written in SQL over a column or over the whole <literal>properties</literal> bag.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DROP INDEX</command> is wider. It takes any index of a label, an index over
   properties included, under the schema qualified name. So an index over properties can be
   dropped by either statement, whichever statement made it, while this one stays inside the
   set it owns.
  </para>

  <para>
   An index that belongs to a constraint is not dropped here. It goes with the constraint,
   through <xref linkend="cypher-dropconstraint"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-droppropertyindex-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error for an index that is not there. Other names in the
      same statement are still dropped.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Unqualified name of the property index to drop. Several names may be given, separated
      by commas, and they need not be on the same label.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CASCADE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Drop the objects that depend on the index.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RESTRICT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refuse if anything depends on the index. This is the default. An index rarely has
      dependents, so the two behave alike most of the time.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-droppropertyindex-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build a graph of their own and drop it at the end:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH unindexed;
SET graph_path = unindexed;
CREATE VLABEL movie;
CREATE VLABEL person;
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_idx ON movie (title);
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX movie_year_idx ON movie (year);
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX person_name_idx ON person (name);
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Drop one index:
<programlisting>
DROP PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_idx;

DROP PROPERTY INDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A second attempt is an error, because the index is gone:
<programlisting>
DROP PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_idx;

ERROR:  index "movie_title_idx" does not exist
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> turns that into a notice:
<programlisting>
DROP PROPERTY INDEX IF EXISTS movie_title_idx;

NOTICE:  index "movie_title_idx" does not exist, skipping
DROP PROPERTY INDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Several names go in one statement, and they need not belong to the same label:
<programlisting>
DROP PROPERTY INDEX movie_year_idx, person_name_idx;

DROP PROPERTY INDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The name cannot carry the graph:
<programlisting>
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_idx ON movie (title);
DROP PROPERTY INDEX unindexed.movie_title_idx;

ERROR:  improper property index name
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An index the statement does not own is refused. A label's primary key is one:
<programlisting>
DROP PROPERTY INDEX movie_pkey;

ERROR:  "movie_pkey" is not property index
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DROP INDEX</command> takes the same index, under its schema qualified name:
<programlisting>
DROP INDEX unindexed.movie_title_idx;

DROP INDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH unindexed CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-droppropertyindex-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   Because the name is resolved in <varname>graph_path</varname>, an index that exists in
   another graph reads as missing rather than as belonging to someone else. Two graphs may
   hold indexes of the same name, so check <literal>\dGi</literal> before assuming which one
   a bare name will reach.
  </para>

  <para>
   To turn a label's indexes off without dropping them, for a bulk load say, use
   <literal>ALTER VLABEL <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> DISABLE
   INDEX</literal> and <xref linkend="cypher-reindex"/> instead. That keeps the definitions
   and only invalidates them.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-droppropertyindex-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no index DDL. Its catalog modifying statements reach only schemas,
   graphs and graph types, in clause 12, so this statement is an AgensGraph extension.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DROP PROPERTY INDEX</command> corresponds to <command>DROP INDEX</command>, and
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal>, the name list and
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> or <literal>RESTRICT</literal> behave as they do there. It
   differs in three ways. The name is resolved in <varname>graph_path</varname> rather than
   <varname>search_path</varname> and may not be schema qualified.
   <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> is not accepted. And the two statements do not reach the
   same set: <command>DROP INDEX</command> takes any index of a label, while this statement
   takes only an index over properties.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

